 * \ue200cite\ue20237\u2020numpy.linalg.qr\ue201 L46:       * \ue200cite\ue20238\u2020numpy.linalg.svd\ue201 L47:       * \ue200cite\ue20239\u2020numpy.linalg.svdvals\ue201 L48:       * \ue200cite\ue20240\u2020numpy.linalg.eig\ue201 L49:       * \ue200cite\ue20241\u2020numpy.linalg.eigh\ue201 L50:       * \ue200cite\ue20242\u2020numpy.linalg.eigvals\ue201 L51:       * \ue200cite\ue20243\u2020numpy.linalg.eigvalsh\ue201 L52:       * \ue200cite\ue20244\u2020numpy.linalg.norm\ue201 L53:       * \ue200cite\ue20245\u2020numpy.linalg.matrix_norm\ue201 L54:       * \ue200cite\ue20246\u2020numpy.linalg.vector_norm\ue201 L55:       * \ue200cite\ue20247\u2020numpy.linalg.cond\ue201 L56:       * \ue200cite\ue20248\u2020numpy.linalg.det\ue201 L57:       * \ue200cite\ue20249\u2020numpy.linalg.matrix_rank\ue201 L58:       * \ue200cite\ue20250\u2020numpy.linalg.slogdet\ue201 L59:       * \ue200cite\ue20251\u2020numpy.trace\ue201 L60:       * \ue200cite\ue20252\u2020numpy.linalg.trace\ue201 L61:       * \ue200cite\ue20253\u2020numpy.linalg.solve\ue201 L62:       * \ue200cite\ue20254\u2020numpy.linalg.tensorsolve\ue201 L63:       * \ue200cite\ue20255\u2020numpy.linalg.lstsq\ue201 L64:       * \ue200cite\ue20256\u2020numpy.linalg.inv\ue201 L65:       * \ue200cite\ue20257\u2020numpy.linalg.pinv\ue201 L66:       * \ue200cite\ue20258\u2020numpy.linalg.tensorinv\ue201 L67:       * \ue200cite\ue20259\u2020numpy.diagonal\ue201 L68:       * \ue200cite\ue20260\u2020numpy.linalg.diagonal\ue201 L69:       * \ue200cite\ue20261\u2020numpy.linalg.matrix_transpose\ue201 L70:       * \ue200cite\ue20262\u2020numpy.linalg.LinAlgError\ue201 L71:     * \ue200cite\ue20263\u2020Logic functions\ue201 L72:     * \ue200cite\ue20264\u2020Masked array operations\ue201 L73:     * \ue200cite\ue20265\u2020Mathematical functions\ue201 L74:     * \ue200cite\ue20266\u2020Miscellaneous routines\ue201 L75:     * \ue200cite\ue20267\u2020Polynomials\ue201 L76:     * \ue200cite\ue20268\u2020Random sampling (`numpy.random`)\ue201 L77:     * \ue200cite\ue20269\u2020Set routines\ue201 L78:     * \ue200cite\ue20270\u2020Sorting, searching, and counting\ue201 L79:     * \ue200cite\ue20271\u2020Statistics\ue201 L80:     * \ue200cite\ue20272\u2020Test support (`numpy.testing`)\ue201 L81:     * \ue200cite\ue20273\u2020Window functions\ue201 L82:   * \ue200cite\ue20274\u2020Typing (`numpy.typing`)\ue201 L83:   * \ue200cite\ue20275\u2020Packaging (`numpy.distutils`)\ue201 L84: \nL85:   * \ue200cite\ue20276\u2020NumPy C-API\ue201 L86: \nL87:   * \ue200cite\ue20277\u2020Array API standard compatibility\ue201 L88:   * \ue200cite\ue20278\u2020CPU/SIMD optimizations\ue201 L89:   * \ue200cite\ue20279\u2020Global state\ue201 L90:   * \ue200cite\ue20280\u2020NumPy security\ue201 L91:   * \ue200cite\ue20281\u2020Status of `numpy.distutils` and migration advice\ue201 L92:   * \ue200cite\ue20282\u2020`numpy.distutils` user guide\ue201 L93:   * \ue200cite\ue20283\u2020NumPy and SWIG\ue201 L94: # numpy.linalg.lstsq\ue200cite\ue20284\u2020#\ue201 L95: \nL96: linalg.lstsq(a, b, rcond=None)\ue200cite\ue20285\u2020[source]\u2020github.com\ue201 \ue200cite\ue20286\u2020#\ue201 L97: \nL98: \nL99: Return the least-squares solution to a linear matrix equation.\nL100: Computes the vector x that approximately solves the equation `a @ x = b`. The equation may be under-, well-, or over-determined (i.e., the number of linearly independent rows of a can be less than, equal to, or greater than its number of linearly independent columns). If a is square and of full rank, then x (but for round-off error) is the \u201cexact\u201d solution of the equation. Else, x minimizes the Euclidean 2-norm \\(||b - ax||\\).\nL101: If there are multiple minimizing solutions, the one with the smallest 2-norm \\(||x||\\) is returned.\nL102: Parameters:\nL103: \nL104: \nL105: a(M, N) array_like\nL106: \nL107: \nL108: \u201cCoefficient\u201d matrix.\nL109: \nL110: b{(M,), (M, K)} array_like\nL111: \nL112: \nL113: Ordinate or \u201cdependent variable\u201d values. If b is two-dimensional, the least-squares solution is calculated for each of the K columns of b.\nL114: \nL115: rcond float, optional\nL116: Cut-off ratio for small singular values of a. For the purposes of rank determination, singular values are treated as zero if they are smaller than rcond times the largest singular value of a. The default uses the machine precision times `max(M, N)`. Passing `-1` will use machine precision.\nL117: \nL118: Changed in version 2.0: Previously, the default was `-1`, but a warning was given that this would change.\nL119: \nL120: Returns:\nL121: \nL122: \nL123: x{(N,), (N, K)} ndarray\nL124: Least-squares solution. If b is two-dimensional, the solutions are in the K columns of x.\nL125: \nL126: residuals{(1,), (K,), (0,)} ndarray\nL127: \nL128: \nL129: Sums of squared residuals: Squared Euclidean 2-norm for each column in `b - a @ x`. If the rank of a is < N or M <= N, this is an empty array. If b is 1-dimensional, this is a (1,) shape array. Otherwise the shape is (K,).\nL130: \nL131: rank int\nL132: \nL133: \nL134: Rank of matrix a.\nL135: \nL136: s(min(M, N),) ndarray\nL137: \nL138: \nL139: Singular values of a.\nL140: \nL141: Raises:\nL142: \nL143: \nL144: LinAlgError\nL145: \nL146: \nL147: If computation does not converge.\nL148: See also\nL149: \nL150: \ue200cite\ue20287\u2020`scipy.linalg.lstsq`\u2020docs.scipy.org\ue201 L151: \nL152: \nL153: Similar function in SciPy.\nL154: \nL155: Notes\nL156: \nL157: If b is a matrix, then all array results are returned as matrices.\nL158: \nL159: Examples\nL160: \nL161: Fit a line, `y = mx + c`, through some noisy data-points:\nL162: \nL163:     >>> x = np.array([0, 1, 2, 3])\nL164:     >>> y = np.array([-1, 0.2, 0.9, 2.1])\nL165: \nL166: \nL167: By examining the coefficients, we see that the line should have a gradient of roughly 1 and cut the y-axis at, more or less, -1.\nL168: We can rewrite the line equation as `y = Ap`, where `A = [[x 1]]` and `p = [[m], [c]]`. Now use \ue200cite\ue20286\u2020`lstsq`\ue201 to solve for p:\nL169: \nL170:     >>> A = np.vstack([x, np.ones(len(x))]).T\nL171:     >>> A\nL172:     array([[ 0.,  1.],\nL173:            [ 1.,  1.],\nL174:            [ 2.,  1.],\nL175:            [ 3.,  1.]])\nL176: \nL177: \nL178:     >>> m, c = np.linalg.lstsq(A, y)[0]\nL179:     >>> m, c\nL180:     (1.0 -0.95) # may vary\nL181: Plot the data along with the fitted line:\nL182: \nL183:     >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nL184:     >>> _ = plt.plot(x, y, 'o', label='Original data', markersize=10)\nL185:     >>> _ = plt.plot(x, m*x + c, 'r', label='Fitted line')\nL186:     >>> _ = plt.legend()\nL187:     >>> plt.show()\nL188: \nL189: \nL190: \ue200cite\ue20288\u2020Image: ../../_images/numpy-linalg-lstsq-1.png\ue201 L191: \nL192: On this page\nL193: \nL194: \u00a9 Copyright 2008-2024, NumPy Developers.\nL195: \nL196: Created using \ue200cite\ue20289\u2020Sphinx\u2020www.sphinx-doc.org\ue201 7.2.6.\nL197: \nL198: Built with the \ue200cite\ue20290\u2020PyData Sphinx Theme\u2020pydata-sphinx-theme.readthedocs.io\ue201 0.15.3.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n4.1.4.1. Linear Least Squares Regression (https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section1/pmd141.htm)\n\ue200cite\ue202turn11view2\ue201 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section1/pmd141.htm\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 31\nL0: \ue200cite\ue2020\u2020Image\ue201 L1: 4. \ue200cite\ue2021\u2020Process Modeling\ue201 L2: 4.1. \ue200cite\ue2022\u2020Introduction to Process Modeling\ue201 L3: 4.1.4. \ue200cite\ue2023\u2020What are some of the different statistical methods for model building?\ue201 L4: \nL5: ## 4.1.4.1.\nL6: ## Linear Least Squares Regression\nL7: Modeling Workhorse  | Linear least squares regression is by far the most widely used modeling method. It is what most people mean when they say they have used \"regression\", \"linear regression\" or \"least squares\" to fit a model to their data. Not only is linear least squares regression the most widely used modeling method, but it has been adapted to a broad range of situations that are outside its direct scope.\nL8: It plays a strong underlying role in many other modeling methods, including the other methods discussed in this section: \ue200cite\ue2024\u2020nonlinear least squares regression\ue201 , \ue200cite\ue2025\u2020weighted least squares regression\ue201 and \ue200cite\ue2026\u2020LOESS\ue201 .\nL9: Definition of a Linear Least Squares Model  | Used directly, with an \ue200cite\ue2027\u2020appropriate data set\ue201 , linear least squares regression can be used to fit the data with any function of the form $$ f(\\vec{x};\\vec{\\beta}) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2x_2 + \\ldots $$ in which\nL10:   1. each explanatory variable in the function is multiplied by an unknown parameter,\nL11:   2. there is at most one unknown parameter with no corresponding explanatory variable, and\nL12:   3. all of the individual terms are summed to produce the final function value.\nL13: In statistical terms, any function that meets these criteria would be called a \"linear function\". The term \"linear\" is used, even though the function may not be a straight line, because if the unknown parameters are considered to be variables and the explanatory variables are considered to be known coefficients corresponding to those \"variables\", then the problem becomes a system (usually overdetermined) of linear equations that can be solved for the values of the unknown parameters.\nL14: To differentiate the various meanings of the word \"linear\", the linear models being discussed here are often said to be \"linear in the parameters\" or \"statistically linear\".\nL15: Why \"Least Squares\"?  | Linear least squares regression also gets its name from the way the estimates of the unknown parameters are computed. The \"method of least squares\" that is used to obtain parameter estimates was independently developed in the late 1700's and the early 1800's by the mathematicians Karl Friedrich Gauss, Adrien Marie Legendre and (possibly) Robert Adrain \ue200cite\ue2028\u2020[Stigler (1978)]\ue201 \ue200cite\ue2029\u2020[Harter (1983)]\ue201 \ue200cite\ue20210\u2020[Stigler (1986)]\ue201 working in Germany, France and America, respectively.\nL16: In the least squares method the unknown parameters are estimated by minimizing the sum of the squared deviations between the data and the model. The minimization process reduces the overdetermined system of equations formed by the data to a sensible system of \\(p\\), (where \\(p\\) is the number of parameters in the functional part of the model) equations in \\(p\\) unknowns. This new system of equations is then solved to obtain the parameter estimates.\nL17: To learn more about how the method of least squares is used to estimate the parameters, see \ue200cite\ue20211\u2020Section 4.4.3.1\ue201 .\nL18: Examples of Linear Functions  | As just mentioned above, linear models are not limited to being straight lines or planes, but include a fairly wide range of shapes. For example, a simple quadratic curve, $$ f(x;\\vec{\\beta}) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1x + \\beta_{11}x^2 \\, ,$$ is linear in the statistical sense.\nL19: A straight-line model in \\(\\log(x)\\), $$ f(x;\\vec{\\beta}) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1\\ln(x) \\, , $$ or a polynomial in \\(\\sin(x)\\), $$ f(x;\\vec{\\beta}) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1\\sin(x) + \\beta_2\\sin(2x) + \\beta_3\\sin(3x) \\, , $$ is also linear in the statistical sense because they are linear in the parameters, though not with respect to the observed explanatory variable, \\(x\\).\nL20: Nonlinear Model Example  | Just as models that are linear in the statistical sense do not have to be linear with respect to the explanatory variables, nonlinear models can be linear with respect to the explanatory variables, but not with respect to the parameters. For example, $$ f(x;\\vec{\\beta}) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_0\\beta_1x $$ is linear in \\(x\\), but it cannot be written in the general form of a linear model presented \ue200cite\ue20212\u2020above\ue201 .\nL21: This is because the slope of this line is expressed as the product of two parameters. As a result, nonlinear least squares regression could be used to fit this model, but linear least squares cannot be used. For further examples and discussion of nonlinear models see the next section, \ue200cite\ue2024\u2020Section 4.1.4.2\ue201 .\nL22: Advantages of Linear Least Squares  | Linear least squares regression has earned its place as the primary tool for process modeling because of its effectiveness and completeness.\nL23:  | Though there are types of data that are better described by functions that are nonlinear in the parameters, many processes in science and engineering are well-described by linear models. This is because either the processes are inherently linear or because, over short ranges, any process can be well-approximated by a linear model.\nL24:  | The estimates of the unknown parameters obtained from linear least squares regression are the optimal estimates from a broad class of possible parameter estimates under the usual assumptions used for process modeling. Practically speaking, linear least squares regression makes very efficient use of the data. Good results can be obtained with relatively small data sets.\nL25:  | Finally, the theory associated with linear regression is well-understood and allows for construction of different types of easily-interpretable statistical intervals for predictions, calibrations, and optimizations. These statistical intervals can then be used to give clear answers to scientific and engineering questions.\nL26: Disadvantages of Linear Least Squares  | The main disadvantages of linear least squares are limitations in the shapes that linear models can assume over long ranges, possibly poor extrapolation properties, and sensitivity to outliers.\nL27:  | Linear models with nonlinear terms in the predictor variables curve relatively slowly, so for inherently nonlinear processes it becomes increasingly difficult to find a linear model that fits the data well as the range of the data increases. As the explanatory variables become extreme, the output of the linear model will also always more extreme. This means that linear models may not be effective for extrapolating the results of a process for which data cannot be collected in the region of interest.\nL28: Of course extrapolation is potentially dangerous regardless of the model type.\nL29:  | Finally, while the method of least squares often gives optimal estimates of the unknown parameters, it is very sensitive to the presence of unusual data points in the data used to fit a model. One or two outliers can sometimes seriously skew the results of a least squares analysis. This makes \ue200cite\ue20213\u2020model validation\ue201 , \ue200cite\ue20214\u2020especially with respect to outliers\ue201 , critical to obtaining sound answers to the questions motivating the construction of the model.\nL30: \ue200cite\ue20215\u2020Image\ue201 "